# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): researched card by card from the sources below,
# each card checked against at least two of them, then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://www.wikidata.org/> ,
              <https://archive.org/details/Fsi-FrenchBasicCourserevised-StudentText> ,
              <https://fr.wiktionary.org/> ,
              <https://en.wiktionary.org/> ,
              <https://sv.wiktionary.org/> ,
              <https://github.com/chrplr/openlexicon/tree/master/datasets-info/Lexique383> ,
              <https://folkets-lexikon.csc.kth.se/folkets/> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ,
                       <#review-5> ,
                       <#review-6> ,
                       <#review-7> ,
                       <#review-8> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the comments on this compilation, its review rounds and its sources are condensed from the deck's dossier, the working record kept while the deck was compiled, from which a provenance report was generated. The dossier's per-card evidence and Wikidata checks, its numbered queries and scripts (its Queries section) and its logs of discrepancies are not part of this release; where the text refers to them, to the dossier or the report, or to findings listed below, it means that record. The builder is the script that built the deck from the dossier and ran its Wikidata checks."@en ,
                 "The cards were checked by machine (live Wikidata and the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators)."@en ,
                 "Word list. A candidate list of 120 words and phrases for a first French course was drawn up by hand from the brief's categories (numbers 0–20, the tens and 100; days; months; colours; family; greetings and polite phrases; everyday nouns; adjectives; verbs), choosing the most basic word for each concept, the same as the first authored Spanish deck where the languages allow. No frequency list or textbook word list was copied; Lexique 3's frequency columns were not consulted."@en ,
                 "Wikidata lexemes. SPARQL query 1 fetched every French lexeme (language Q150) whose lemma is a candidate, with its category, gender (P5185), senses, English and French glosses and sense items (P5137). Six candidates were not found: quatre-vingts and five phrases. The lexeme search (API calls 2) found quatre-vingt (L10500, lemma without s) but no lexeme for au revoir, à bientôt, s'il vous plaît, je vous en prie, excusez-moi or de rien (je vous en prie was searched in review round 3, see call 2; it too returns no lexeme). So the five phrases on the cards without a lexeme are au revoir, à bientôt, s'il vous plaît, je vous en prie and excusez-moi; de rien, a sixth phrase without one, is not a card. The full JSON of the lexeme chosen for each word (the numeral lexeme for numbers, the noun lexeme for nouns, the adjective lexeme for colours and adjectives, and so on) was fetched with API calls 3, and the English and Swedish lexemes named by the senses' translation statements (P5972) with API calls 4."@en ,
                 "Wikidata items. The items linked from the chosen senses (P5137), plus four items found with the item search (API calls 6: good evening Q19279366, goodbye Q2506912, hello Q12068060, thank you Q19279214), were fetched with API calls 5 (labels and aliases in fr, en, sv and mul). They gave the English and Swedish names of days, months, colours, family members, greetings and everyday things and the digit labels of the numbers. Where a sense has several items, the one for the card's meaning was used (eau: Q283 water; porte: sense S3, Q36794 door, not S1, Q53060 gate)."@en ,
                 "Phrases from the FSI course. For the five phrases with no Wikidata lexeme, the Internet Archive was searched for public-domain Foreign Service Institute French courses (call 8) and the OCR text of the French Basic Course (Revised), volumes 1 and 2, was downloaded (call 9). Au revoir, à bientôt, excusez-moi (Unit 1 dialogue), s'il vous plaît (Unit 1, classroom expressions, and a Unit 5 drill) and je vous en prie (Unit 2 dialogue, the reply to Merci beaucoup, glossed \"You're welcome\") were found with grep (call 10) and read in context with the English column. De rien does not occur as a phrase in either volume (only in sentences such as Je n'ai besoin de rien), nor in the FSI Metropolitan French FAST course, whose text was also searched (call 11). The deck's reply to merci is je vous en prie, the reply the public-domain course gives; de rien, the more casual reply with the same meaning, is named in its note rather than given a card of its own, since its back would be the same (the authors' knowledge of usage, confirmed by French Wiktionary). FSI's OCR text has no accents; the accented spellings come from French Wiktionary and Lexique."@en ,
                 "Writing the cards. The front is the French word as in the lexeme's lemma (or FSI's phrase), lower case. Nouns are preceded by the definite article that shows their gender (le livre, la maison). Convention for elided articles: before a vowel or mute h the article is written l' and the gender is added in brackets, m. or f. (l'eau (f.), l'école (f.), l'homme (m.), l'ami (m.)); the apostrophe is the straight ASCII apostrophe, as in s'il vous plaît. Days and months are given without an article, as usually written, with their gender in a note. Colours and adjectives are given in the masculine singular with the feminine in a note; verbs in the infinitive. The back is our own short English and Swedish rendering of the main sense: for nouns the bare noun, for verbs the infinitive (English \"to …\", Swedish \"att …\"), for numbers the digits (tagged zxx, as digits are in no language). English and Swedish follow the Wikidata labels where an item exists and has them; otherwise they are the usual equivalents, chosen by the authors and checked in English Wiktionary, Folkets lexikon and Swedish Wiktionary (step 8). Where a French word has two everyday English or Swedish equivalents both are given (fille: daughter, girl; grand: big, tall; aller: gå, åka; aimer: love, like). Rule for text not given by a content source: English and Swedish equivalents and the facts behind the notes come from the authors' own knowledge of the languages, are worded by the authors and are only confirmed in the verification sources; such single words and short facts of usage count as the authors' own content. Where a back coincides with a verification source's gloss, the enwiktionary source record says so. Swedish backs are chosen in the register of the French: s'il vous plaît, which is polite, is \"vänligen\" (Folkets: särskilt i artighetsuttryck), not the colloquial \"snälla\" (Swedish Wiktionary: vardagligt) (review round 1)."@en ,
                 "Notes. A short note was added where it helps a beginner and is confirmed by a source: the gender of each day and month (shown for days with the indefinite article, un lundi, since le lundi means on Mondays), the feminine of each colour and adjective (nouvel and vieil before a masculine singular noun beginning with a vowel or mute h, confirmed by the French and English Wiktionary pages nouvel and vieil), un as the indefinite article, neuf also meaning new (with a front note \"number\" on neuf and un, and \"colour\" on orange), soixante-dix, quatre-vingts and quatre-vingt-dix with the Belgian and Swiss septante and nonante, quatre-vingt without s before another number, the pronunciation of fils, femme also meaning wife, homme also a human being, l'amie, la livre (pound), bonjour by day and bonsoir in the evening, salut and the tu/vous forms s'il te plaît and excuse-moi, de rien, Swedish requests ending with tack and snälla being colloquial, and the Swedish gå/åka distinction. Notes are our own wording."@en ,
                 "Cross-checking. Every French word and phrase was looked up in French Wiktionary (calls 13): spelling, part of speech, the gender mark on the head line against the article on the card, and the first relevant definition (quoted in each card's evidence). Every word was also looked up in Lexique 3.83 (call 16) for spelling, category and gender. Every English meaning was checked against the French section of English Wiktionary (calls 14). Every Swedish word was checked in Folkets lexikon (call 17), English → Swedish, or Swedish → English when the first direction had no match, and, where a page exists, in the French entry of Swedish Wiktionary (calls 15). A Python script built the evidence lines from the responses saved during authoring, so each quote is what the source returned, with wiki markup and footnote references removed (the parsing logic is given with the calls); quotations longer than 160 characters are cut and marked with \"…\". The raw responses and scripts were working files and are not published."@en ,
                 "Dictionaries not used. The brief suggested the Dictionnaire de l'Académie française and Larousse for checking the French. Their terms were read first: the Académie's conditions (https://www.dictionnaire-academie.fr/CGU) forbid \"d’utiliser tout système automatisé ou logiciel pour aspirer ou extraire des données du Site\", and Larousse's (https://www.larousse.fr/infos/cgu) state that it \"s’oppose à toutes opérations de moissonnage et de fouille de textes et de données\", including automated collection. Before reading them, the authoring agent had made a few test requests to find out how the sites work (Académie: the page https://www.dictionnaire-academie.fr/search?term=maison, its script /js/scriptAF.js, two POST requests to /search with term=maison, and the entry https://www.dictionnaire-academie.fr/article/A9M0242; Larousse: https://www.larousse.fr/dictionnaires/francais/maison); their content was not used for any card, and neither dictionary was consulted further. French Wiktionary and Lexique 3, whose licences allow automated use, took their place."@en ,
                 "Machine checks. The dossier's Wikidata checks (the French, English and Swedish label or alias of each linked item that matches the card's text, and the mul digit label of each number) are re-run against live Wikidata by the builder on every build. Wikidata lexemes cannot be checked by the builder (it checks items only); the lexeme facts in the evidence were read from the API responses of 4 October 2026. The built deck was validated with the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators (scripts/validate_sources.ts)."@en ,
                 "Excluded: de rien as a card of its own (it means the same as je vous en prie, the reply in the public-domain FSI course, and is named in that card's note), words with two equally common meanings that a single card cannot separate (le temps: time or weather; la glace: ice or mirror; la pièce: room or coin), brown and purple (marron or brun; violet or mauve), words whose everyday form differs between France, Belgium, Switzerland and Canada beyond the tens noted on the cards (déjeuner, dîner, souper), the formal/informal pairs tu and vous (which need grammar, not a flashcard), the year (an and année, two words for one concept), and connaître/savoir (both to know)."@en ,
                 "Selection: 120 cards: the cardinal numbers 0–20, the tens 30–90 and 100 (29); the seven days of the week; the twelve months; eight colours (red, blue, green, yellow, black, white, grey, orange); eight family members (mother, father, brother, sister, son, daughter, grandfather, grandmother); twelve greetings and polite phrases (bonjour, bonsoir, bonne nuit, salut, au revoir, à bientôt, merci, s'il vous plaît, je vous en prie, excusez-moi, oui, non); 22 everyday nouns (house, water, bread, milk, apple, book, table, chair, door, window, dog, cat, town, street, school, man, woman, friend, hand, day, night, week); six common adjectives (grand, petit, bon, mauvais, nouveau, vieux); and 16 common verbs in the infinitive (être, avoir, aller, faire, venir, parler, manger, boire, aimer, vouloir, pouvoir, voir, lire, écrire, dormir, travailler). The list was chosen by the deck's authors for a first course, not copied from any frequency list or textbook. Every word has a Wikidata lexeme except the five phrases noted in the method, which come from the public-domain FSI course. Left out: de rien as a card of its own (same meaning as je vous en prie, which the FSI course gives as the reply to merci; it is named in that card's note), words with two equally common meanings (temps, glace, pièce), brown and purple (several competing words), words for meals whose meaning differs between France, Belgium, Switzerland and Canada, tu/vous, the pair an/année, and connaître/savoir. Nouns are given with the definite article; before a vowel or mute h the elided l' is followed by the gender, (m.) or (f.). Colours and adjectives are given in the masculine singular, with the feminine in a note. Days and months are given without an article and in lower case, as French writes them; their gender is in a note on every day and month card. Numbers are the standard forms of France (soixante-dix, quatre-vingts, quatre-vingt-dix), with the Belgian and Swiss septante and nonante in notes."@en ,
                 "Licensing: The deck is CC0 1.0. Its content comes from two sources whose terms allow any reuse: Wikidata's lexemes and items (CC0 1.0: French lemmas, genders, forms, glosses, translations and French, English and Swedish labels) and the Foreign Service Institute's French Basic Course (Revised), a work of the United States Government with no copyright protection under 17 U.S.C. § 105 (five phrases with their meaning, and a check of four more). The selection of words is the authors' own. The English and Swedish renderings not taken from Wikidata are the usual equivalents chosen by the authors; several of them are the same short stock glosses that English Wiktionary, Swedish Wiktionary or Folkets lexikon give, as the method says. Single words and fixed greetings such as \"see you soon\" or \"ingen orsak\" are facts of usage, not protectable expression, so this does not carry those sources' licences into the deck; no definition, example, comment or list was taken from them. The rule applied throughout: English and Swedish equivalents and the facts in the notes not given by a content source are the authors' own knowledge, worded by them and only confirmed in a verification source. The backs that coincide with English Wiktionary's glosses are listed in its source record; two longer ones that matched it word for word (the back of pouvoir and a second sense in the note on je vous en prie) were replaced or removed in review round 3. French Wiktionary, English Wiktionary and Swedish Wiktionary (CC BY-SA 4.0), Lexique 3.83 (CC BY-SA 4.0) and Folkets lexikon (CC BY-SA 2.5) were used for verification only. The Dictionnaire de l'Académie française and Larousse were not used, because their terms forbid automated extraction (method, step 9). So no source requires attribution or share-alike for the deck, although all are credited in its provenance. The quotations in the provenance report are not covered by the deck's CC0 dedication or the repository's MIT licence: quotations from the three Wiktionaries are © their contributors, CC BY-SA 4.0; quotations from Lexique 3.83 are CC BY-SA 4.0; quotations from Folkets lexikon are CC BY-SA 2.5; quotations from the FSI course are in the public domain; Wikidata data is CC0."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Sources, licences and machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 120 cards: every French form, gender, English and Swedish meaning and note against the content sources and the verification sources; the builder's Wikidata checks."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Every card has evidence from a content source (Wikidata, or the FSI course for five phrases) and at least two verification sources. French Wiktionary and Lexique agree with the spelling of every French word; the gender shown on every noun, day and month card agrees with the Wikidata lexeme, French Wiktionary and, where it records one, Lexique. English Wiktionary confirms every English back; Folkets lexikon, Swedish Wiktionary or a Swedish Wikidata label confirms every Swedish back. The builder's Wikidata checks cover the French, English and Swedish labels or aliases of 98 linked items (259 checks), including the digit label of every number; they all pass. Discrepancies between sources and how they were resolved are listed below. No review round has been run yet."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Factual accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 120 cards: French spellings, genders (by an independent SPARQL query over all nouns, days and months), numbers, meanings and notes; the builder's Wikidata checks re-run live."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The review found no factual errors in the cards' main facts: every gender matches the lexeme, every spelling and number is right and the builder's 259 checks pass. Three warnings and two suggestions were raised. The notes on nouveau and vieux left out that nouvel and vieil are masculine singular only and are also used before a mute h; the Swedish back of s'il vous plaît, snälla, is colloquial while the French is polite; the description credited Wikidata with meanings it does not give; and the checks on voir and nouveau confirm little. All were verified against the sources and four were fixed; the fifth needed no change."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Language, translation and language tags"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 120 cards and the deck metadata in French, English and Swedish, against the dossier and the built TTL, including every language tag."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The review found the French, English and Swedish correct and every language tag right (29 zxx numeral backs, every front fr, no untagged card text). One warning: the description put the day and month gender note on the front, while it is a back note. Five suggestions: mention mute h in the elision convention, better literal glosses for quatre-vingts and quatre-vingt-dix, un lundi rather than le lundi in the day notes, a register hint for snälla, and untagged keywords in the TTL. Four were fixed, one was already fixed in round 1 and one is outside the dossier."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing, attribution and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Every source's licence page, the licensing paragraph, the wording of all 120 backs and notes against the sources, the evidence of about 20 cards, and the method, queries and source records."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The review confirmed every recorded licence and the CC0 dedication overall, but found four English backs or notes that match English Wiktionary's glosses without disclosure, an inconsistent rule for facts confirmed only by verification sources (de rien), two wrong FSI locators, and three documentation slips. All were verified. The pouvoir back and the je vous en prie note were changed, the remaining coinciding glosses are disclosed, one rule is now stated in the method and licensing, and the locators, counts and query record were corrected; the deck stays CC0."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Re-check of changed cards and a sample (facts, language, documentation)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 15 cards changed in round 3's fixes, every third card in dossier order (44 distinct cards in all), the builder's Wikidata checks (120 cards, 259 checks), the SHACL and DCAT-AP validation, and the deck's metadata and documentation."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The review found the cards correct and two documentation errors: one FSI locator still said \"introduction\" and method step 1 still said only round 0 had been done. It also made three suggestions. All five were verified against the sources (call 24) and applied."@en .

<#review-5>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Re-check of changed cards and a sample (facts, language, documentation)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The 3 cards changed in round 4 (non, s-il-vous-plait, bonjour), every third card in dossier order (42 cards in all), the builder's Wikidata checks (120 cards, 259 checks), the SHACL and DCAT-AP validation, and the deck's metadata and documentation."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The review found no errors or warnings and confirmed every round 4 fix. Its one suggestion, that the published report numbered each method step twice, was verified in the report and applied."@en .

<#review-6>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Final full-deck review (facts and language)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 120 cards (fronts, backs, notes, language tags), the description and selection counts, the round 4 and 5 fixes, and a live run of the builder's Wikidata checks (120 cards, 259 checks)."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The review found every card correct, unambiguous and atomic, confirmed the earlier fixes and the counts in the description, and made one suggestion on the punctuation of the Swedish description, which was applied."@en .

<#review-7>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Version 2: title, description and keywords in Korean, German, Spanish and French (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the deck’s title, description and keywords, translated by an AI agent from the English (the Swedish settling what was ambiguous) into Korean, German, Spanish and French, in the words the app itself uses, and checked by a second AI agent that translated every text back into English and fixed what had drifted; the release compared by a script with version 1, which found the new title, description and keywords and the release’s own metadata the only changes, and checked by the library checker; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: the title, description and keywords are in Korean, German, Spanish and French as well; no card changed."@en .

<#review-8>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Version 3: meanings and notes in Korean, German and Spanish (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: every card’s back and note, the word’s meaning,, translated by AI agents from the English (the Swedish settling what was ambiguous) into Korean, German and Spanish, and checked by a second AI agent that translated every text back into English and fixed what had drifted; the release compared by a script with the version before, which found the new texts, the description, the languages and the release’s own metadata the only changes, and checked by the library checker; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: every back and note is in Korean, German and Spanish as well; the fronts, the English and Swedish meanings and the sources are unchanged."@en .

